      "text": "To solve the problem \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), we will use a known trigonometric identity and properties of tangent functions. The identity we will use is:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}\n\\]\n\nLet's set \\(A = 20^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 25^\\circ\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\tan 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\tan 45^\\circ = 1\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = 1\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by \\(1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\). Expanding this product, we get:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\n\nUsing the equation \\(\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1\\), we substitute this into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1 + 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
